Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Euclidean Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell["\<\
Abstract written and reviewed by Pavel Hajek on 18 October 2026.\
\>", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell["\<\
Euclidean Infrageometry studies natural geometric objects on arbitrary graph \
substrates using synthetic methods. It enumerates these objects, studies \
their mutual relations, applies operations to them, and performs iterative \
constructions.\
\>", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "If space is represented by a large graph, which we may view as a web of \
paths, human geometry can be viewed as an idealized theory based on our \
large-scale perception of infinitesimality. This leads to the idealization of \
space as a ",
 StyleBox["\"continuous web\"",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " supporting idealized objects such as the \"breadthless line.\" In \
contrast, Infrageometry works directly with the discrete substrate at an \
observer's scale and ",
 StyleBox["aggregates instead of subdivides",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Consequently, objects at the least scale may not be unique, leading to ",
 StyleBox["multi-objects",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 ", so that constructions generate branching multiway systems. Although \
concrete representatives may differ across branches, the system is ",
 StyleBox["\"causally invariant\"",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " in the sense that the dependency graph of construction steps is the same \
in every branch. We associate each multi-object with vertex and edge \
densities that count the representatives containing each vertex or edge, \
obtaining a measure by dividing by the total number of representatives. We \
expect that, under a suitable notion of Gromov-Hausdorff convergence, these \
measures converge weakly to Dirac measures supported on classical \"thin\" \
geometric objects. We also expect that a suitable axiomatization of Euclidean \
infrageometry can be formulated and the notion of  Euclidean-like graphs can \
be defined so that subdivision and convergence converges to the continuous \
axiomatic Euclidean geometry."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We use inert objects such as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraSegment"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSegment", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["q", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "4c463fbe-d5c6-4c1d-9328-cfa6faf69733"],
 ", independent of a graph substrate. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraMeasurement"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraMeasurement", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["obj", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["property", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "cf37407c-3989-4c65-86bd-716f0e9c6dfc"],
 " then computes a property of an object on a concrete graph. Ambiguous \
objects also have named samplers; for example, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["RandomInfraSegment"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraSegment", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"InfraSegment", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", " ", 
      StyleBox["q", "TI"]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["k", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "a7957224-6987-42d3-b8fc-0353b17baf05"],
 " gives up to ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " shortest paths from ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " to ",
 StyleBox["q", "TI"],
 " as vertex lists."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell["\<\
Segments, rays, lines and arcs have compact representations as directed \
acyclic graphs such that the chains correspond to individual representatives. \
This allows densities to be computed efficiently without enumerating every \
path.\
\>", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Inspired by ",
 ButtonBox["GeometricScene",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://reference.wolfram.com/language/ref/GeometricScene.html"], 
    None}],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraScene"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraScene", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "901a13aa-a613-4adb-9653-3e53cf83caef"],
 " lets us describe a construction through named objects and hypotheses. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["RandomInfraSceneInstance"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraSceneInstance", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["scene", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["graph", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "1abfed2e-76ba-43ab-8c27-be06371455fd"],
 " draws an admissible instance on a given substrate, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraSceneViewer"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSceneViewer", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["scene", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["graph", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "cac1c387-30e6-4b2d-a1c7-7a79e92e4000"],
 " displays the construction step by step in both the causal and branchial \
directions."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

